WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.320 --> 00:00:05.420
Zinc gutters drain rainwater and in the
process develop a natural resistance to

00:00:05.420 --> 00:00:12.050
corrosion. When zinc is exposed to wet and
dry cycles, a chemical reaction occurs,

00:00:12.050 --> 00:00:15.200
forming a protective patina on its surface.

00:00:15.410 --> 00:00:18.110
No paint or special treatment needed.

00:00:18.380 --> 00:00:23.030
You can't stop the rain, but you can stop it
from damaging buildings and their

00:00:23.030 --> 00:00:28.850
foundations. These U shaped zinc gutters
funnel water away from the structures to keep

00:00:28.850 --> 00:00:35.570
them dry. The process starts with the
production of these 46 ton coils of zinc.

00:00:36.320 --> 00:00:40.190
The zinc arrives at the factory in the form
of thick plates.

00:00:40.700 --> 00:00:44.120
These plates are almost 100% zinc.

00:00:46.250 --> 00:00:48.500
The plates drop into a furnace.

00:00:48.500 --> 00:00:52.550
Inside, the temperature reaches 750°F.

00:00:52.580 --> 00:00:58.100
Workers add a bit of copper and titanium to
reinforce the zinc, and it melts into a

00:00:58.100 --> 00:00:59.480
homogeneous liquid.

00:01:01.730 --> 00:01:06.020
The molten zinc then flows into a trough
style conveyor system.

00:01:07.550 --> 00:01:13.160
Residues like ashes float to the top of the
metal soup, and a worker skims them off.

00:01:15.850 --> 00:01:21.160
A floating puck monitors the speed and depth
of the zinc blende to prevent overflow.

00:01:23.330 --> 00:01:25.880
The molten zinc now arrives at the casting
machine.

00:01:27.410 --> 00:01:33.320
Here, three foot wide steel unwinds and once
linear, the zinc flows onto it.

00:01:34.130 --> 00:01:38.570
The steel acts as a mould to form the zinc
into a long rigid sheet.

00:01:41.150 --> 00:01:45.740
Water flows onto the zinc to accelerate
cooling and solidification.

00:01:46.520 --> 00:01:51.890
Since water and liquid zinc don't mix, the
water simply runs off the surface.

00:01:53.990 --> 00:02:00.380
Now solid, the zinc plate is almost a third
of an inch thick, far too thick for making

00:02:00.380 --> 00:02:01.940
lightweight rain gutters.

00:02:02.090 --> 00:02:04.700
The next operation will thin the plate down
dramatically.

00:02:07.280 --> 00:02:09.620
This is a powerful rolling mill.

00:02:09.860 --> 00:02:14.690
The pressure on its rollers is so great that
they often must be replaced.

00:02:16.280 --> 00:02:20.270
Once the new rollers have been loaded, the
mill heats them up.

00:02:21.140 --> 00:02:25.070
The operator activates the rollers as they
revolve.

00:02:25.850 --> 00:02:31.730
The hot rollers exert 770 tons of pressure
to iron the zinc much thinner.

00:02:33.260 --> 00:02:38.540
Meanwhile, just ahead, a steel belt
tensioning system swings into action.

00:02:39.320 --> 00:02:44.630
This system prevents slackening in the zinc
sheet as it exits the rolling mill and moves

00:02:44.630 --> 00:02:48.890
forward. You can see the difference the
rolling has made.

00:02:48.890 --> 00:02:53.060
It has reduced the thickness of the zinc
sheet to about three quarters of a

00:02:53.060 --> 00:03:00.320
millimeter. The steel belt system maintains
the tension in the zinc sheet as it's wound

00:03:00.320 --> 00:03:01.430
around a core.

00:03:01.970 --> 00:03:05.510
This produces the 46 ton coil of zinc.

00:03:05.930 --> 00:03:08.090
It's known as the mother coil.

00:03:08.120 --> 00:03:12.770
Approximately 25 miles of rain gutters can
be made from this coil.

00:03:14.180 --> 00:03:16.970
Workers slit the mother coil into three.

00:03:17.000 --> 00:03:19.130
Each coil is a foot wide.

00:03:20.210 --> 00:03:23.900
It is now the correct width to be formed
into rain gutters.

00:03:26.410 --> 00:03:30.370
A crane lifts one of the narrower coils over
the factory floor.

00:03:30.430 --> 00:03:33.550
Its journey is guided by the operator below.

00:03:35.470 --> 00:03:40.750
This puts it in position to be mounted to
the next machine, one that will cut it into

00:03:40.750 --> 00:03:43.270
uniform 13 foot long strips.

00:03:43.660 --> 00:03:45.730
That's the standard length for the rain
gutters.

00:03:47.290 --> 00:03:52.900
As the zinc uncoils, an overhead mirror
provides a top view for the operator.

00:03:55.480 --> 00:03:56.830
In quick swoops.

00:03:56.830 --> 00:04:00.400
The blade slices the zinc sheeting to the
correct length.

00:04:02.500 --> 00:04:07.360
The zinc strips then travel through a
progressively narrow channel and over a

00:04:07.360 --> 00:04:10.360
cylinder. This rounds the profile.

00:04:12.370 --> 00:04:17.440
More rollers, improve on the U-shape and
also curl the edges of the gutters to

00:04:17.440 --> 00:04:18.550
reinforce them.

00:04:21.350 --> 00:04:24.020
This gives the zinc rain gutters their final
shape.

00:04:26.290 --> 00:04:31.630
The automated profiler ejects the 13 foot
long gutters onto a rounded rack.

00:04:31.840 --> 00:04:36.100
Then it tips to stack the gutters neatly on
top of each other.

00:04:37.420 --> 00:04:40.180
The zinc rain gutters are ready to hang.

00:04:40.420 --> 00:04:45.670
On site, they'll be welded together and
attach to the roof with special hooks.

00:04:47.110 --> 00:04:49.960
They should come in handy on a rainy day.

00:04:50.230 --> 00:04:55.100
The French Basque Country is culturally
quite distinct from the rest of France.

00:04:55.120 --> 00:05:00.820
Located in the southwest bordering Spain,
the region is known, among other things, for

00:05:00.820 --> 00:05:06.940
its ham, which is salted and air cured in an
old tradition passed down through generations

00:05:06.940 --> 00:05:08.290
of Basque farmers.

00:05:08.440 --> 00:05:11.530
There are several types of traditional
Basque ham.

00:05:11.680 --> 00:05:17.800
This one, called quinoa, is marbled with fat
and has a deep, strong, fruity flavor.

00:05:18.130 --> 00:05:23.320
Its distinct taste and composition begin
with a specific breed of pig.

00:05:23.350 --> 00:05:27.670
The Basque Country piebald refers to the
animal's skin.

00:05:28.810 --> 00:05:33.970
This pig breed is native to the Basque
region, where livestock are raised on small

00:05:33.970 --> 00:05:36.430
farms in the valleys of the Pyrenees
mountains.

00:05:38.440 --> 00:05:43.900
Unlike commercially farmed animals, the
female pigs have small litters of 6 to 9

00:05:43.900 --> 00:05:49.240
piglets which grow up roaming and grazing
along the vast mountainsides.

00:05:49.270 --> 00:05:52.180
On an average of two acres of land per 30
piglets.

00:05:53.170 --> 00:05:58.960
As a result of this free range rearing, the
pigs grow more slowly and become heavier than

00:05:58.960 --> 00:06:00.730
commercially farmed pigs.

00:06:02.780 --> 00:06:08.780
Once the animals reach a weight of about
£330, which usually takes a year to 15

00:06:08.780 --> 00:06:11.780
months, they go to the slaughterhouse.

00:06:12.680 --> 00:06:16.160
Each carcass is stamped with an
identification number.

00:06:16.940 --> 00:06:20.000
Traditional ham is made from the hind legs.

00:06:21.140 --> 00:06:27.020
After tagging the leg, a worker places it in
a machine which brands the canto a symbol

00:06:27.020 --> 00:06:28.160
into the flesh.

00:06:32.160 --> 00:06:36.540
Then another worker spreads dry salt on the
exposed portion.

00:06:36.840 --> 00:06:42.270
The salt comes from Sayda debayan, a
saltwater spring in a basin bordered by the

00:06:42.270 --> 00:06:43.410
Pyrenees Mountains.

00:06:43.440 --> 00:06:46.170
Theodora River and the Atlantic Ocean.

00:06:49.130 --> 00:06:55.850
Workers leave the salted ham in a cold, damp
room for one day per every £2, typically

00:06:55.850 --> 00:06:57.950
about 15 days per ham.

00:06:58.190 --> 00:07:00.380
The salt preserves the meat.

00:07:00.560 --> 00:07:04.070
Next, the ham undergoes a first dry curing.

00:07:05.240 --> 00:07:09.890
It hangs on a stainless steel rack in a
temperature and humidity controlled room for

00:07:09.890 --> 00:07:10.970
seven months.

00:07:14.120 --> 00:07:19.850
When the ham comes out of the room, it has
lost about 20% of its original weight because

00:07:19.850 --> 00:07:22.100
most of the moisture has evaporated.

00:07:24.320 --> 00:07:26.510
A worker brushes off the surface.

00:07:30.180 --> 00:07:35.460
And coats the exposed part of the ham with a
special grease that's made out of pigs

00:07:35.460 --> 00:07:38.340
kidneys, rice flour and water.

00:07:42.520 --> 00:07:47.350
This grease prevents the surface from drying
out and crusting during the second dry

00:07:47.380 --> 00:07:55.390
curing. The ham now goes into a second
drying room, which dries the ham in the open

00:07:55.390 --> 00:08:01.930
air. Taking advantage of this particular
geographic location, a warm southern wind

00:08:01.930 --> 00:08:07.780
from Spain and humid air from the Atlantic
Ocean to the west blow into the room through

00:08:07.780 --> 00:08:10.840
windows. Nature does all the work.

00:08:11.170 --> 00:08:16.870
Humans merely regulate the airflow and
humidity by lifting or lowering the blinds.

00:08:18.460 --> 00:08:23.200
In this room, the ham hangs from a wooden
rather than stainless steel rack.

00:08:23.230 --> 00:08:28.360
That's because the wood absorbs excess
humidity and transfers moisture to the meat

00:08:28.360 --> 00:08:30.100
if the air gets too dry.

00:08:32.700 --> 00:08:39.270
As the ham dries, its flavor concentrates
the Charcutier, an artisanal pork butcher,

00:08:39.270 --> 00:08:41.610
inspects every ham carefully.

00:08:46.300 --> 00:08:47.380
The wind that dries.

00:08:47.380 --> 00:08:52.750
The ham carries pollen and other natural
particles, which give this ham a distinct

00:08:52.750 --> 00:08:54.490
flavor and fragrance.

00:08:56.330 --> 00:09:01.670
The secret is slow curing until the ham
reaches optimal maturity.

00:09:01.700 --> 00:09:07.850
That takes approximately one month per £2,
so the ham remains in the second drying room

00:09:07.850 --> 00:09:09.200
for more than a year.

00:09:10.100 --> 00:09:15.740
When the cured ham is ready, workers
sprinkle it with espelette pepper, a mildly

00:09:15.740 --> 00:09:18.050
hot pepper from the northern Basque Country.

00:09:19.040 --> 00:09:23.780
This gives the ham a fragrance that's as
appetizing as its taste.

